{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Breakouts: The model consistently fails to capture upward momentum spikes (e.g., the jump from ~85 to ~93), forecasting a linear drift while true prices surge. Apply a positive bias adjustment of +4 to +6 EUR when the latest history shows a sharp single-day increase (>3 EUR) regardless of low sentiment scores.",
    "Lag in Mean Reversion: The model reacts too slowly to price peaks, predicting a slow decline while actual prices oscillate with higher amplitude. When the forecast predicts a monotonic decrease following a spike, apply a correction to flatten the curve and introduce volatility matching the historical range.",
    "Sentiment Ignorance: The model ignores sentiment history (which shows intermittent positive signals of 0.20-0.33) and treats all inputs as neutral. Incorporate a sentiment multiplier: if recent sentiment > 0.20, increase the forecast slope by 15-20% to anticipate accelerated growth.",
    "Horizon-Specific Overshoot Correction: The model's 30-day horizon is too smooth, missing the secondary peaks (e.g., ~100 EUR) that occur 10-15 days after the initial breakout. Adjust the mid-term forecast (days 10-20) upward by 5-7 EUR to account for the delayed secondary rally.",
    "Volatility Underestimation: The forecast range is artificially narrow compared to the true outcome's variance. Widen the forecast confidence interval by 20% to reflect the observed high volatility in the EU ETS market during this period."
  ]
}
